    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d268">

                <pb facs="0315" n="1149" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp01v02/pg1149"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 657.119/387a</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State to the Minister in
                        Norway</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Schmedeman</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-03-09T18:00:00-05:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">March 9, 1918—6 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>255. At request of Doctor Nansen War Trade Board have issued export licenses to
                    Norwegian Food Commission for shipment via <pb facs="0316" n="1150"
                        xml:id="pg1150"/> S.S. <hi rendition="#italic">Bergensfjord</hi> sailing
                    March 9 covering approximately 156 tons of seeds destined for spring planting in
                    Norway. The War Trade Board have given the question of the issuance of those
                    licenses very careful thought and have caused them to be issued because of
                    political and other considerations, particularly the imminence of the conclusion
                    of this country’s agreement with Norway and because no other sailings after
                    March 9 were sufficiently proximate.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Polk</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
